    "parsing_res_list": [
        {
            "block_label": "header",
            "block_content": "HEAT CONDUCTION EQUATION",
            "block_bbox": [
                75,
                5,
                441,
                47
            ],
            "block_id": 0,
            "block_order": null
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "## EXAMPLE 2–11 A Wall with Various Sets of Boundary Conditions",
            "block_bbox": [
                549,
                61,
                1364,
                153
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": 1
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Consider steady one-dimensional heat conduction in a large plane wall of thickness L and constant thermal conductivity k with no heat generation. Obtain expressions for the variation of temperature within the wall for the following pairs of boundary conditions (Fig. 2–43):",
            "block_bbox": [
                542,
                151,
                1534,
                314
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 2
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ (a)-k\\frac{dT(0)}{dx}=\\dot{q}_{0}=40\\ W/cm^{2}\\qquad and\\qquad T(0)=T_{0}=15^{\\circ}C $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                553,
                314,
                1346,
                389
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 3
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ (b)-k\\frac{dT(0)}{dx}=\\dot{q}_{0}=40\\ W/cm^{2}\\qquad and\\qquad-k\\frac{dT(L)}{dx}=\\dot{q}_{L}=-25\\ W/cm^{2} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                550,
                380,
                1507,
                466
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 4
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ (c)-k\\frac{dT(0)}{dx}=\\dot{q}_{0}=40\\ W/cm^{2}\\qquad and\\qquad-k\\frac{dT(L)}{dx}=\\dot{q}_{L}=\\dot{q}_{0}=40\\ W/cm^{2} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                548,
                463,
                1537,
                546
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 5
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "SOLUTION Steady one-dimensional heat conduction in a large plane wall is considered. The variation of temperature is to be determined for different sets of boundary conditions.",
            "block_bbox": [
                528,
                596,
                1544,
                704
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 6
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Analysis This is a steady one-dimensional heat conduction problem with constant thermal conductivity and no heat generation in the medium, and the heat conduction equation in this case can be expressed as (Eq. 2–17)",
            "block_bbox": [
                526,
                704,
                1551,
                815
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 7
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ \\frac{d^{2}T}{dx^{2}}=0 $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                969,
                848,
                1091,
                926
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 8
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "whose general solution was determined in the previous example by direct integration to be",
            "block_bbox": [
                518,
                963,
                1562,
                1039
            ],
            "block_id": 9,
            "block_order": 9
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ T(x)=C_{1}x+C_{2} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                919,
                1060,
                1148,
                1099
            ],
            "block_id": 10,
            "block_order": 10
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "where  $ C_{1} $  and  $ C_{2} $  are two arbitrary integration constants. The specific solutions corresponding to each specified pair of boundary conditions are determined as follows.",
            "block_bbox": [
                510,
                1121,
                1575,
                1231
            ],
            "block_id": 11,
            "block_order": 11
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "(a) In this case, both boundary conditions are specified at the same boundary at x = 0, and no boundary condition is specified at the other boundary at x = L. Noting that",
            "block_bbox": [
                507,
                1250,
                1582,
                1361
            ],
            "block_id": 12,
            "block_order": 12
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ \\frac{dT}{dx}=C_{1} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                973,
                1384,
                1101,
                1461
            ],
            "block_id": 13,
            "block_order": 13
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "the application of the boundary conditions gives",
            "block_bbox": [
                507,
                1489,
                1171,
                1530
            ],
            "block_id": 14,
            "block_order": 14
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ -k\\frac{dT(0)}{dx}=\\dot{q}_{0}\\quad\\rightarrow\\quad-kC_{1}=\\dot{q}_{0}\\quad\\rightarrow\\quad C_{1}=-\\frac{\\dot{q}_{0}}{k} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                706,
                1545,
                1396,
                1637
            ],
            "block_id": 15,
            "block_order": 15
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "and",
            "block_bbox": [
                512,
                1644,
                575,
                1678
            ],
            "block_id": 16,
            "block_order": 16
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ T(0)=T_{0}\\quad\\rightarrow\\quad T_{0}=C_{1}\\times0+C_{2}\\quad\\rightarrow\\quad C_{2}=T_{0} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                709,
                1721,
                1396,
                1779
            ],
            "block_id": 17,
            "block_order": 17
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Substituting, the specific solution in this case is determined to be",
            "block_bbox": [
                515,
                1780,
                1429,
                1843
            ],
            "block_id": 18,
            "block_order": 18
        },
        {
            "block_label": "display_formula",
            "block_content": " $$ T(x)=-\\frac{\\dot{q}_{0}}{k}x+T_{0} $$ ",
            "block_bbox": [
                920,
                1852,
                1183,
                1936
            ],
            "block_id": 19,
            "block_order": 19
        }
    ],
